```

All three DROP rules match **inbound traffic on eth2** (the backbone link to router3) — 24 ICMP packets (2016 bytes) already counted. The only exception is TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1, which has 0 hits. PC16 can reach its gateway (router6, 172.18.51.254) fine, and router6 itself can reach PC13 — but any traffic that arrives at router6 **from the backbone (eth2)** destined for 172.18.51.x, and any **ICMP replies** returning from the backbone for packets sent by PC15–PC18, are silently dropped here. This is a confirmed `acl-skill` fault.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The iptables FORWARD chain on router6 contains three DROP rules that block all TCP, UDP, and ICMP traffic arriving on eth2 (the backbone-facing interface toward router3 via the r6_r3 link). Only one narrow ACCEPT rule exists ahead of the DROPs: TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1, which has zero hits.

**Mechanism:** Router6 is the sole L3 gateway for the 172.18.51.0/24 subnet (eth1 = 172.18.51.254, serving PC15–PC18). All inter-site traffic to/from this subnet must cross eth2 at some point — either as inbound forwarded traffic (backbone → LAN) or as returning ICMP replies (backbone → LAN for replies to packets PC15–PC18 sent outward). Both cases are killed by the DROP rules on eth2. PC16 can reach its gateway (router6) and router6 can reach PC13 directly, but any attempt by PC11–PC14 to reach PC15–PC18 has its return ICMP reply dropped at router6, and any attempt by PC15–PC18 to ping a backbone host times out because the ICMP echo reply re-enters router6 on eth2 and is immediately dropped (24 packets / 2016 bytes observed dropped).

**Localization:** router6, iptables FORWARD chain, eth2 ingress DROP rules (lines 2–4 of the FORWARD chain).

**Root cause:** Overly restrictive iptables FORWARD chain rules on router6 drop all ICMP, TCP, and UDP traffic entering from the backbone interface (eth2), effectively severing bidirectional reachability between the 172.18.51.0/24 segment and every other network.

**Fix:** Remove (or selectively replace) the three DROP rules on router6's FORWARD chain matching `-i eth2`: `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`, and `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p icmp -j DROP`. Also persist the corrected ruleset so it survives a restart.
=== END ===